  "dddeac84-4207-4aa5-b2fa-705eb54026af": {
   "id": "dddeac84-4207-4aa5-b2fa-705eb54026af",
   "message": {
    "id": "dddeac84-4207-4aa5-b2fa-705eb54026af",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1777866930.553,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "For a student-telemetry dataset, reviewers will focus on these. Answer them concretely, not with boilerplate:\n\n**Collection & consent**\n- Who were the subjects, how were they recruited, was consent opt-in, how many declined, IRB protocol number/exemption.\n- Were students able to withdraw later? Is there a process now?\n\n**De-identification**\n- Exactly what was removed or hashed (names, emails, free-text keystrokes outside code, file paths with usernames).\n- Can windows be linked back to a student? (Pseudonymous student IDs are *necessary* for grouped splits, so explain they're random and unlinkable.)\n- Re-identification risk from code itself (unique solutions, comments with names). What you did about it.\n\n**Labels**\n- Who labeled, training, guide version, agreement (and whether it was computed pre-adjudication).\n\n**Intended & prohibited uses**\n- Explicitly: not for grading, admissions, or monitoring individual students. That's the question R3-type reviewers always ask.\n\n**Recommended splits**\n- Provide the official split and say **how** it was made. If it's by window, reviewers who know telemetry will ask about leakage.\n\n**Maintenance & license**\n- Hosting (e.g., institutional repository + Hugging Face mirror), license (CC BY-NC 4.0 is common for student data), contact, versioning."
     ]
    },
